### Tone: Rule slide: the call. As each reveal starts, before the truth lands, every test subject calls whether the truth is
### higher or lower than their own answer. It scores no points; FerMEANie keeps count anyway, for an award at the end.
### A self-assessment exercise, clinically curious about how well humans know their own errors.

rules-call-1 = Before the truth lands, tell me: is it higher or lower than your answer? No points. I simply want to know if you know.
rules-call-2 = Each reveal starts with a question for you: higher or lower than your own answer? It scores nothing. It reveals everything.
rules-call-3 = Higher or lower? You get a few seconds to guess which way you were wrong. Not *if*. Which way.
rules-call-4 = Before the truth arrives, you call it: higher or lower than your answer. Zero points. I log it anyway. I log everything.
rules-call-5 = A small self-assessment before each truth: higher, or lower? Get it right often enough and there’s an award. Get it wrong and there’s a spreadsheet.
rules-call-6 = You will be asked whether the truth is above or below your own estimate. It’s free. Like most of your guesses, it’s also optional.
rules-call-7 = Higher or lower than your answer? Call it before the truth lands. No points, just the quiet dignity of having known.
rules-call-8 = Every reveal opens with one tap: higher or lower. It costs nothing and pays nothing. I find it **very** informative.
rules-call-9 = Knowing you’re wrong is one skill. Knowing which way is another. Before each truth, show me the second one.
rules-call-10 = Before I reveal the truth, you guess its direction. Higher, or lower. Whoever calls it right most often gets an award. Hindsight, with a head start.
rules-call-11 = Quick calibration before each truth: higher or lower than your answer? No points. Just data. My favourite kind of reward.
rules-call-12 = Tap higher or lower before the truth lands. It doesn’t change your score. It changes how I file you.
